WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:08.179
This is a quick demonstration of recent enhancements
in our Genome Data Viewer, including several

00:00:08.179 --> 00:00:13.080
track graph settings and the ability to apply
settings to all tracks in a composite Track

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.580
Hub.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.100
Even though today's examples use the Genome
Data Viewer, these enhancements also apply

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:27.250
to other NCBI resources that incorporate the
Sequence Viewer, such as SNP and Gene.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:32.480
Starting on the GDV home page, use the Browse
genome button to go into the current human

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.580
assembly, GRCh38.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:41.390
Then in the Tracks menu, use Configure Track
Hubs, and connect to the Blueprint epigenomics

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:49.200
hub,
which you see is a composite track.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.190
I've already selected a few of the bigWig
tracks, but notice that you can adjust some

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.600
track settings right here in this hub configuration
menu,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.820
including an option to apply these settings
to the entire group.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:04.809
I'll hit Configure,
and these tracks load in the Viewer.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:11.070
I used the search widget to go to the region
of the rcc1 gene, mostly just to zoom in a

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.070
bit.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.409
And let's say you want a cleaner view of the
peaks in this lower track, which now has a

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:21.820
maximum value of 4.3 in the default linear
scale.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.740
Click on the track name, or you can also just
hover on the track name then click on the

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.050
gear icon in the tooltip menu that appears.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:38.470
I'll change to a line graph, and set the value
range, from 1 to 4 for this linear scale.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.000
Notice that this menu includes the option
to apply these settings to the entire Blueprint

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.840
group.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:54.610
We even indicate where data have been clipped
in the newly set range - note the red region.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.450
To show a few more new features, I'll go back
to the Configure Track Hubs menu to connect

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:05.090
to a different hub and select two tracks that
display as a merged track.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.420
Here is that merged track, which combines
data for both splice donor and splice acceptor

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.750
predictions, in green and red, respectively.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:20.640
But you can now separate them into individual
tracks by choosing the Stacked view.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.500
You can also change the color scheme, in what
we call a subtrack.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.240
If I click on the green side, the menu opens
with that track selected.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.410
But you can can change the color and other
subtrack settings for both tracks in this

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.700
menu.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.020
You can also hide one of the tracks.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:50.070
To recover that subtrack, go back in to the
menu, select the hidden subtrack, and uncheck

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:56.140
Hide subtrack.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.930
That's it for this demonstration.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.310
You can review these new features in a post
to our blog, NCBI Insights,

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.230
ncbiinsights.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.220
And as always, we'd love to have your feedback
and questions.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:11.150
Feedback is right here, and for questions,
please use our Support Center.

